<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pierre Dugua de Mons</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1558–1628</span> · Also known as Sieur de Mons</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pierre Dugua de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mons">Mons</a> was a Huguenot merchant and nobleman from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saintonge">Saintonge</a> who held the French royal monopoly of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. In 1604 he led the expedition, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-de-champlain">Samuel de Champlain</a>, that wintered on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-croix">Saint Croix</a> Island, and in 1605 he founded Port-Royal, the first lasting French settlement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acadia">Acadia</a>. He also sponsored Champlain's founding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> in 1608.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pierre-dugua-de-mons.jpg" alt="Pierre Dugua de Mons" width="246" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pierre_Dugua,_Sieur_de_Mons.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
